===Etymology===
According to Etymology Online, the term was first attested in 1706 meaning {{m|en|[[am]] [[not]]}}, and it was used with that sense until the early 19th century, when it began to be used as a generic contraction for {{m|en|[[are]] [[not]]}}, {{m|en|[[is]] [[not]]}}, etc. in the [[w:Cockney|Cockney dialect]]. It was then "popularized by representations of London cockney dialect in Dickens, etc., which led to the word being banished {{...}} from correct English".<ref>{{R:Etymonline|ain't}}</ref>

The shift from {{IPAchar|/ænt/|lang=en}} to {{IPAchar|/eɪnt/|lang=en}} parallels a similar change some dialects made to {{m|en|can't}}. In other dialects, the pronunciation shifted to {{IPAchar|/ɑːnt/|lang=en}}, and the spelling {{m|en|aren't}}, when used to mean “am not”, is due to the fact that both words are pronounced {{IPAchar|/ɑːnt/|lang=en}} in some non-[[rhotic]] dialects. Historically, {{m|en|ain't}} was present in many dialects of the English language, but not in the southeastern England dialect that became the standard, where it is only found in the construction {{m|en|ain't I}}.

As a contraction of {{m|en|[[have]] [[not]]}} and {{m|en|[[has]] [[not]]}}, {{m|en|ain't}} derives from the earlier form {{m|en|han't}}, which shifted from {{IPAchar|/hænt/|lang=en}} to {{IPAchar|/heɪnt/|lang=en}}, and underwent ''h''-dropping in most dialects.

